 <!-- FAQ -->
 <section id="faq" class="mb-16">
 <div class="bg-slate-50 rounded-2xl p-8 border border-slate-200 shadow-sm">
 <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-brandBlue uppercase italic mb-8 text-center">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
 <div class="space-y-6 max-w-3xl mx-auto">
 <div class="bg-white rounded-xl p-6">
 <h3 class="font-black text-brandBlue mb-2">What 3 specs should OEM buyers verify on a PD 3.1 charger datasheet?</h3>
 <p class="text-slate-600 text-sm faq-answer">USB-C PD 3.1 introduces <strong>Extended Power Range (EPR)</strong>, raising the maximum from PD 3.0's 100W to <strong>240W (48V × 5A)</strong> through a single USB-C cable. Key additions: three new voltage levels (28V, 36V, 48V), AVS for 100mV fine-tuning above 15V, and mandatory EPR e-marker cables above 100W. PD 3.0 SPR caps at 20V/100W for smartphones and ultrabooks. PD 3.1 EPR covers laptops, workstations, and gaming devices. PD 3.1 chargers are backward-compatible with PD 3.0 devices. By mid-2026, <strong>~60% of new laptops support 140W+ USB-C charging</strong>, and PD 3.1 adoption exceeds 79-82%.</p>
 </div>
 <div class="bg-white rounded-xl p-6">
 <h3 class="font-black text-brandBlue mb-2">What wattage tier should OEM brands choose, 65W, 100W, 140W, or 240W?</h3>
 <p class="text-slate-600 text-sm faq-answer"><strong>65W (20V):</strong> baseline for ultrabooks, lowest cost, highest volume. <strong>100W (20V):</strong> covers ~95% of commercial laptops, FOB +15-20% vs 65W. <strong>140W (28V): the B2B sweet spot</strong> for gaming/performance laptops in 2026, MacBook Pro 16", Dell XPS, ThinkPad P-series, FOB <strong>~$12-16/unit at MOQ 500</strong>. 240W (48V): niche workstation tier, only ~12% of shipments. PD 3.0 SPR (65-100W) covers volume at FOB $4-9/unit. For most brands: PD 3.0 for volume SKUs, PD 3.1 140W for flagship.</p>
 </div>
 <div class="bg-white rounded-xl p-6">
 <h3 class="font-black text-brandBlue mb-2">What cable do OEM brands need for PD 3.1 240W charging?</h3>
 <p class="text-slate-600 text-sm faq-answer">PD 3.1 EPR above 100W requires <strong>EPR-rated USB-C 2.1 cables with e-marker chip</strong> certified for the charger's max voltage. 20-60W = standard 3A. 65-100W = 5A e-marked ($1-2 FOB). <strong>140-240W = EPR e-marker rated for 240W ($2-4 FOB).</strong> Without the correct cable, the charger auto-downgrades to 60W regardless of its rating. Standard 100W cables cannot safely support EPR. Always bundle the matching cable in-box, a 140W charger with a 60W cable generates negative reviews and returns. Verify EPR cable specs at usb.org.</p>
 </div>
 <div class="bg-white rounded-xl p-6">
 <h3 class="font-black text-brandBlue mb-2">Does the EU mandate require PD 3.1 for laptops?</h3>
 <p class="text-slate-600 text-sm faq-answer">The <strong>EU Common Charger Directive 2022/2380 mandates USB-C with USB PD for all laptops from April 28, 2026</strong>, making PD 3.1 the de facto standard for any laptop charger above 100W. While the directive doesn't explicitly name PD 3.1, laptops needing over 100W can only use PD 3.1 EPR. EU Ecodesign 2025/2052 requires ≥87% active efficiency and ≤0.5W standby, thresholds that effectively mandate GaN-based PD 3.1 designs above 65W. USB-A-only chargers and proprietary ports are now non-compliant for EU-bound products.</p>
 </div>
 <div class="bg-white rounded-xl p-6">
 <h3 class="font-black text-brandBlue mb-2">What three specs must OEM buyers verify when sourcing PD 3.1 chargers from factories?</h3>
 <p class="text-slate-600 text-sm faq-answer"><strong>(1) EPR support</strong>, charger must deliver 28V/36V/48V (not just 20V SPR), confirmed via USB-IF TID at usb.org. <strong>(2) AVS compliance</strong>, required for voltage control above 15V. <strong>(3) EPR e-marker cable pairing</strong>, bundled cable must carry an EPR e-marker chip rated for max voltage. Without all three = defaults to 60W. Also demand: <strong>test reports dated 2025+</strong> (early 2023-2024 modules had EPR bugs with Dell/HP), 100% functional testing + aging under full load, and IEC 62368-1 reports from accredited labs (SGS, TÜV, Intertek).</p>
 </div>
 <div class="bg-white rounded-xl p-6">
 <h3 class="font-black text-brandBlue mb-2">What certifications are required for PD 3.1 chargers in global markets?</h3>
 <p class="text-slate-600 text-sm faq-answer"><strong>EU:</strong> CE (LVD+EMC), IEC 62368-1:2023, Ecodesign 2025/2052 (≥87% efficiency, ≤0.5W standby), IEC 62680-1-2 USB-IF interoperability. <strong>US:</strong> FCC, DOE/CEC Level VI. <strong>Strongly recommended:</strong> USB-IF TID certification, ENEC safety mark, EPR interoperability test reports from 2025+. Multi-market parallel certification (US+EU+UK): budget <strong>$8,000-15,000 total</strong>, saves 3-4 months vs sequential. WOWOHCOOL provides complete USB-IF TID documentation with every PD 3.1 OEM order.</p>
 </div>
 <div class="bg-white rounded-xl p-6">
 <h3 class="font-black text-brandBlue mb-2">What is the difference between PPS and AVS in PD 3.1?</h3>
 <p class="text-slate-600 text-sm faq-answer"><strong>PPS (PD 3.0):</strong> adjusts voltage in 20mV steps, 3.3V to 21V, ideal for smartphone fast charging (Samsung Super Fast Charging, iPhone 27W). <strong>AVS (PD 3.1):</strong> adjusts in 100mV steps, 15V to 48V, designed for laptops and high-power devices, extends battery lifespan by <strong>20-30%</strong> vs fixed-voltage charging. PPS = precision at low voltage; AVS = range at high voltage. Many PD 3.1 chargers support both. For OEM: any charger above 65W should include AVS; any targeting smartphones should include PPS.</p>
 </div>
 <div class="bg-white rounded-xl p-6">
 <h3 class="font-black text-brandBlue mb-2">How do OEM buyers source PD 3.1 GaN chargers from Shenzhen factories?</h3>
 <p class="text-slate-600 text-sm faq-answer"><strong>Step 1:</strong> Choose wattage, 65-100W PD 3.0 for volume, 140W PD 3.1 EPR for flagship. <strong>Step 2:</strong> Verify USB-IF TID at usb.org before deposit. <strong>Step 3:</strong> Demand EPR interoperability reports dated 2025+, early modules had known bugs. <strong>Step 4:</strong> Specify dynamic power allocation for multi-port chargers (updates ~50ms), static allocation causes voltage drops. <strong>Step 5:</strong> Confirm bundled cable matches charger's max voltage. Leading GaN manufacturers offer MOQ from 200 pcs. 140W GaN: <strong>~$12-16/unit FOB at MOQ 500</strong>, factory-direct eliminates 25-35% markup. <strong>WOWOHCOOL provides PD 3.1 OEM from 65W to 240W with USB-IF certification. <a href="/contact/" class="text-brandBlue hover:text-brandOrange underline">Request PD 3.1 sourcing consultation.</a></strong></p>
 </div>
